  "minutes": 22,
  "title": {
    "en": "Female dabbling ducks"
  },
  "subtitle": {
    "en": "Mallard, Gadwall, Wigeon, Pintail, Teal, Garganey and Shoveler: the brown ones"
  },
  "intro": {
    "en": "Every duck in this lesson is a female, and that is the point. From a distance a flock of female dabbling ducks is a mass of mottled brown. The males are easy: you learned them in the beginners course. The females are a different matter, and they decide whether you are a duck birder or not.\n\nThe rules: size and shape first (the head, the bill, the neck), then the bill colour and pattern, then the face (eyebrow, eye-stripe, spot at the bill base) and last the wing patch, the speculum, if she opens a wing. In winter, from October to March, the lakes of north-west Europe are full of ducks from Siberia, Scandinavia and the Baltic. A single flock can hold five of today's species. The Garganey is the odd one out: it is a summer visitor that arrives in March to April and leaves in August.\n\nToday's photos show females only, and every photo is checked against that. In the quiz, the wrong answers are other females, not males."
  },
  "words": [
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "speculum"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "The coloured patch on the wing, usually iridescent, bordered by white lines. Its colour differs by species."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "loral spot"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "A pale spot at the base of the bill, as in the Teal and Garganey female."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "eye-stripe"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "A dark line running back from the eye. In Teal and Garganey it is strong."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "bill pattern"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "The colours on the bill. The female Mallard has an orange bill with a dark blotch on top."
      }
    }
  ],
